llenges frominhaled chemicals *\nor gases,\" including hyperoxia as itis used in clinieat\n\u2018espiratory therapy.\" When damaged ATI cells are\n\u2018eplaced in adult animals, ATH cells apparently func\n\n448,\n\nPUBLICATIONS\n\ntion as progenitors for the new cells\u00bb? *\"\u2122\"* An analy-\nsis ofthis relationship between ATI and ATI cells will\nbe facilitated by the availability of homogeneous popu\nlations of ATI and ATH cells.\" Specifically. unique bi-\n\u2018ochemical markers for each cell type could Be idemtified,\n\u2018thus providing criteria by which to monitor the expres:\nsion of the differentiated state and factorsthat influence\niin an in vitro system. In addition, a possible suscep:\nUbility of ATI cells io pulmonary pathogens can also\nbe specifically investigated in an in vitro system.\n\u2018We therefore have developed an isolation procedure\nfor ATI cells, so that we can characterize these cells.\n\u2018Aficr an initial selective enzymatic digestion of adult\nrat lung by tavaye with 0.2% collagenase, 0.08% tryp-\nsin, 0.008% elastase, and 0.005% DNAse Type 1, the\n\u2018ells that are released are separated by density gradient\n\n_Supparied by Grants 1466 and 1@60A Tiom the C\nfor Tobacco Research.\n\u2018Accepted for publication April 24, 1986.\n\u2018Address reprin requests to Dr. Nancy K. Weller, Depart\n\u2018ment of Pathology, Harvard Medical Schoo!\n25 Shattuck $1, Boston, MA O2115,\n\n018785\n\n10337880\n\npeek CG TRE",
